# Java build output
target/
*.class
!**/src/main/**/target/
!**/src/test/**/target/

# Maven wrapper binary
.mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.jar

# Node
node_modules/
dist/
.vite/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Environment files
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Reports and scanner output
coverage/
dependency-check-report.*
*.sarif

# IDE and editor
.idea/
*.iml
*.iws
*.ipr
.vscode/
.settings/
.project
.classpath
.factorypath
.apt_generated/
.sts4-cache/
.springBeans

# Obsidian vault
.obsidian/

# OS
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store

# Chrome's partial-download files. They appear inside inspiration/ while a reference image or page is
# still downloading and are meaningless once the transfer finishes or is abandoned.
*.crdownload

# The asset folder Chrome writes beside a saved page: minified vendor bundles, tracking pixels and
# stylesheets from whatever site the reference came from. The saved page is kept; a few hundred
# unreviewable third-party files beside it are not.
inspiration/**/*_files/

# Generated by Spring Initializr, not part of the project
HELP.md

# The generated board workbook. FLOW_BOARD.md is "the version in git" by that file's own words, and
# the workbook is derived from the same plan rows. Committing it adds a binary nobody can review and
# an artefact `build-board.py --check` cannot verify: the check compares markdown only, so a
# hand-edited or stale workbook passes the gate green while disagreeing with the plans it claims to
# summarise. Regenerate it with `python scripts/build-board.py` when you want to sort or filter.
work/flow-board.xlsx

# Excel's lock files, created beside a workbook while it is open. They appear the moment somebody
# opens the generated board and vanish when they close it, which is exactly the kind of file that
# gets committed by accident.
~$*

# What the browser suite writes when it fails: a screenshot of the page at the moment of the failing
# assertion, and whatever the run attached to it. Useful while you are looking at a red run, and
# meaningless afterwards — they are pictures of a state that no longer exists, and committing them
# would put a binary nobody can review beside the test that produced it.
app/frontend/src/**/__screenshots__/
app/frontend/.vitest-attachments/

# JVM crash dumps. They appear beside the build when a forked test JVM is killed, and they are
# machine-specific post-mortems of a process that no longer exists.
hs_err_pid*.log
replay_pid*.log

# What a suite run leaves at the repository root when its output is redirected to a file. They are a
# transcript of one run on one machine, they are large, and the only thing anybody wants from them —
# the counts — belongs in the message that reports the run rather than in the tree.
/backend-run.log
/backend-suite.log
/frontend-run.log
/frontend-suite.log

# The corpus's rollback snapshot: a binary image of one PostgreSQL database at one moment, taken so a
# measurement run could be undone. Nobody can review it, nothing reads it but `pg_restore`, and it
# describes a machine rather than the product. The captures under `work/corpus/runs/` are kept and
# this is not, because those are the measurement's evidence and this is its undo button.
work/corpus/backups/
